{
  "abstract": "We study the dense critical core $Q_q=K_{q-3}\\vee C_5$ through its list-coloring relation and its realization in two planar layers. For every $q\\geq4$, lists from one palette of $q$ colors, of size at least $q-1$ at hubs and $\\lfloor q/2\\rfloor+1$ at cycle vertices, admit a proper coloring exactly when their total union is the whole palette. The uniform cycle threshold is sharp, even with full hub lists. The proof selects a singleton and two independent cycle pairs and applies Hall's theorem; the odd-palette case rules out a cyclic chain of complementary lists.\n\nAt nine colors, we also complete the reduction of $Q=K_6\\vee C_5$ in the class of biplanar graphs of maximum degree eleven. A minimum-order non-nine-colorable graph within this class contains no $Q$. The new geometric premise excludes every distribution of the five other hub incidences between one root in each layer when a core layer isolates a hub. A compact standard-library certificate covers 3,190,470 normalized core occurrences and all 5,248 fan pairs, using explicit nonplanarity subdivisions and sphere rotations. Degree-preserving paths and stars then close the previously unresolved three-root case.\n\nThe bounded-class minimality differs from global minimum order, for which a stronger local exclusion was already known. These results sharpen the reusable extension criterion and remove a specific degree-increasing residual; they do not force core occurrence, improve the global chromatic bounds, or resolve Earth–Moon.",
